  "summary": "The Montagnard Hypertension project was an inaugural Community-Based Participatory Research (CBPR) initiative designed to address the high prevalence of hypertension in the Montagnard community. This complex project involved diverse stakeholders—including medical professionals, community health workers, women's groups, tribal elders, church leaders, and youth/students from various backgrounds—and spanned multiple tribal languages (Jarai, Rhade, Bunong). The core goal was multifaceted: to establish baseline data on hypertension, identify underlying risk factors, build trust within the community, and mobilize local resources, particularly the knowledgeable Montagnard youth. Over four phases (2013–2015), the team developed specialized terminology, conducted focus groups, created a culturally relevant behavioral survey, and collected biological and anthropometric data. Crucially, the project was designed to build capacity among Montagnard youth by training them as co-authors and researchers in IRB protocols, literature searches, FGD facilitation, translation/analysis, and survey administration. The mentors often deferred to the cultural expertise of the youth (e.g., creating videos for informed consent). Beyond research skills, the project provided extensive professional development—including internships, scholarships, and career counseling—aimed at positioning these young people as future community leaders. A key finding was the tension between traditional Montagnard leadership expectations and American professional norms. The mentors addressed this by facilitating structured dialogues (using Cultural Translation or CT approaches) between youth and elders to negotiate roles. Ultimately, the project successfully established a sustainable model: the Montagnard Hypertension program evolved into a comprehensive community-led initiative that continues to support youth leadership, cultural preservation, and health advocacy across tribal communities.",
